<h2 id="first-month">How to start a side hustle in San Francisco this month</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Pick a car-free gig if you can.</strong> Skipping gas, parking and insurance is the easiest raise in SF.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose your neighborhoods.</strong> Build your client base in two or three nearby areas so you are not crossing the city for one job.</li>
  <li><strong>Register your business.</strong> Register with the Office of the Treasurer and Tax Collector and note the annual filing deadline at the end of February.</li>
  <li><strong>Get your first reviews.</strong> Rover, Thumbtack, Nextdoor and a Google Business Profile all reward early five-star reviews.</li>
  <li><strong>Plan around the calendar.</strong> Mark major Moscone conferences, Outside Lands, Fleet Week and home games for staffing and delivery surges.</li>
  <li><strong>Price for SF.</strong> Use the <a href="/tools/freelance-rate-calculator/">freelance rate calculator</a> to set rates that cover taxes and downtime.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="sf-taxes">California and San Francisco tax rules</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> California rates run from 1% to 12.3%, plus 1% more on income above $1 million. Pay quarterly estimates to the Franchise Tax Board.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-employment tax:</strong> 15.3% federal on net profit, plus federal income tax. Estimate your set-aside with the <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>AB5:</strong> California's ABC test governs worker classification. If you freelance for businesses, keep multiple clients and your own tools and schedule. App-based drivers and couriers fall under Proposition 22.</li>
  <li><strong>SF business registration:</strong> Required for people doing business in San Francisco, including the self-employed. Registration fees are tiered by San Francisco gross receipts, with $55 as the lowest tier for the 2026–27 year. Since 2026, registration renewal and tax filing are combined in one annual form due by the end of February. Businesses with $5 million or less in San Francisco gross receipts are exempt from the gross receipts tax.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> Selling goods requires a seller's permit from the California Department of Tax and Fee Administration.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><strong>Quick tip:</strong> Scan receipts as you go with a [[amazon:receipt-scanner|portable receipt scanner]] or your phone. E-bike costs, phone bills, supplies and a share of home office expenses can all reduce what you owe.</div>

